1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,520
Syonan Syoken:

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:07,200
The Occupation's Government
Lottery, 1942 to 1945

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,866
On the afternoon of the
10th of September 1942,

4
00:00:10,946 --> 00:00:15,807
seven months into the Japanese
Occupation, a crowd gathered in

5
00:00:15,887 --> 00:00:20,350
the old Victoria Theatre to
watch a lottery being drawn.

6
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:23,678
Women nominated by the
different communities had

7
00:00:23,758 --> 00:00:26,803
been chosen to turn the
drums, and a million

8
00:00:26,883 --> 00:00:30,575
one-dollar tickets were
narrowed down to 62 winners.

9
00:00:30,575 --> 00:00:35,359
The first prize of $100,000
went to a 33-year-old

10
00:00:35,439 --> 00:00:40,600
banker from Kuala Lumpur
who had bought 223 tickets.

11
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,289
It was the first of more than
thirty government lotteries

12
00:00:44,369 --> 00:00:47,662
the Japanese military
administration put on sale in

13
00:00:47,742 --> 00:00:51,431
Singapore and Malaya, and it
was announced from the start

14
00:00:51,511 --> 00:00:54,950
as a way to take money back
out of people's pockets.

15
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:58,026
The lottery was first announced

16
00:00:58,106 --> 00:01:00,550
on the 29th of May 1942.

17
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:04,249
According to a Domei news
report in the Syonan Times,

18
00:01:04,329 --> 00:01:08,813
the Finance Bureau of the military
administration would "shortly

19
00:01:08,893 --> 00:01:12,734
conduct a $1,000,000 lottery"
with "the dual purpose of

20
00:01:12,814 --> 00:01:16,950
increasing revenue and absorbing
excess purchasing power".

21
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:21,782
The tickets would cost $1 each and
be sold at the City Hall and at

22
00:01:21,862 --> 00:01:26,625
the branches of the Yokohama
Specie Bank and the Bank of Taiwan.

23
00:01:26,625 --> 00:01:29,725
The report gave its reasons plainly.

24
00:01:29,725 --> 00:01:34,947
The lottery was "intended to absorb
spending money and inflationary tendencies",

25
00:01:35,027 --> 00:01:38,061
which it blamed on the
British defence building

26
00:01:38,141 --> 00:01:42,303
of the last months before the
fall and on speculation by Chinese

27
00:01:42,383 --> 00:01:46,425
merchants who had pulled their
money out of the stock market.

28
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:50,525
The announcement came at a hard
moment for the city's money.

29
00:01:50,525 --> 00:01:54,588
In the same weeks, the Chinese
community was being made

30
00:01:54,668 --> 00:01:58,883
to raise the $50 million "gift"
demanded by the military,

31
00:01:58,962 --> 00:02:02,800
and on the 25th of June it
handed over a cheque that

32
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,400
included $22 million borrowed
from the Yokohama Specie Bank.

33
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,008
The same bank was named as the place

34
00:02:10,088 --> 00:02:12,775
where lottery winners would be paid.

35
00:02:12,775 --> 00:02:16,523
At the same time the
administration was paying its way

36
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:20,209
with its own new notes, the
currency that came to be

37
00:02:20,289 --> 00:02:23,975
called banana money, printed
in ever larger amounts.

38
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:28,416
A lottery that took in a million
of those dollars and paid out only

39
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:32,950
part of them was one small way of
drawing some of that money back.

40
00:02:32,950 --> 00:02:36,800
The government lottery
was called Syonan Syoken.

41
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:40,802
A notice in the paper on
the 1st of July announced

42
00:02:40,882 --> 00:02:44,394
that the tickets were
on sale from that day,

43
00:02:44,474 --> 00:02:49,211
and an advertisement later that
month put the offer simply:

44
00:02:49,291 --> 00:02:53,700
"Do you want $100,000 or
$50,000 or $10,000 or $1,000?

45
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,057
Buy a ticket in our $1,000,000

46
00:02:56,138 --> 00:02:59,225
government lottery and try your luck."

47
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:03,946
There were 62 prizes, one of $100,000,

48
00:03:04,026 --> 00:03:08,242
one of $50,000, ten of $10,000 and

49
00:03:08,322 --> 00:03:13,250
fifty of $1,000, worth $300,000 in all.

50
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:16,609
The paper was asked to
make it clear that winners

51
00:03:16,689 --> 00:03:20,400
would pay no tax, and that
all prizes would be paid in

52
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:24,200
cash at the local office of
the Yokohama Specie Bank.

53
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:27,608
The draw was first set for
the 10th of August at the

54
00:03:27,688 --> 00:03:32,102
former Victoria Theatre, which had
been renamed for the Occupation,

55
00:03:32,182 --> 00:03:35,200
and was later moved to
the 10th of September.

56
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,019
By then, according to the head
of the finance department,

57
00:03:39,099 --> 00:03:41,755
a Mr Hara, the whole million tickets had

58
00:03:41,835 --> 00:03:45,050
been sold "within a period
of only two months".

59
00:03:45,050 --> 00:03:48,373
In a speech before the draw
he said the lottery had been

60
00:03:48,453 --> 00:03:52,871
"schemed and decided soon after the
termination of hostilities in Malaya",

61
00:03:52,951 --> 00:03:55,362
thanked "the three communities in Malaya,

62
00:03:55,442 --> 00:03:59,434
namely the Chinese, Indians and
the Malays" for their co-operation,

63
00:03:59,514 --> 00:04:03,081
and promised that the money
would be "spent for most fitting

64
00:04:03,161 --> 00:04:05,937
causes such as the relief
of distressed people,

65
00:04:06,017 --> 00:04:10,575
the improvement of sanitary conditions
and other suitable social services".

66
00:04:10,575 --> 00:04:14,450
The winners were reported
over the following days.

67
00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:19,063
The first prize went to S. P.
Letchuman Chettiar of Ampang

68
00:04:19,143 --> 00:04:23,190
Street in Kuala Lumpur,
who had bought 223 tickets,

69
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:28,369
and a few days later the paper
reported that he had given $7,500

70
00:04:28,449 --> 00:04:32,900
of it to a fund for wounded
and sick Japanese soldiers.

71
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,653
The second prize was won by a Sikh

72
00:04:35,733 --> 00:04:38,650
police constable in Negri Sembilan.

73
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:42,240
One of the $10,000 third
prizes went to a dhoby shop

74
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:45,768
on Exeter Road, where it
was shared by ten people,

75
00:04:45,848 --> 00:04:49,800
most of the luck said to have
come from the shop's cook.

76
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:53,839
On the 3rd of October
the Syonan Times reported

77
00:04:53,919 --> 00:04:57,775
what would happen to
the $700,000 left over.

78
00:04:57,775 --> 00:05:02,148
It would be split into four equal
parts, for the Chinese Society to

79
00:05:02,228 --> 00:05:06,733
help the poor, for leper asylums
and mental hospitals, for the Labour

80
00:05:06,813 --> 00:05:11,000
Control Bureau and for the Health
Department's preventive work.

81
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,798
The official who announced
it, a Mr S. Nakahira of

82
00:05:14,878 --> 00:05:18,909
the finance department, said
$700,000 was "not a very

83
00:05:18,989 --> 00:05:22,787
large sum" for so many
organisations and asked the

84
00:05:22,867 --> 00:05:26,900
people of Malaya to support
"any further lotteries".

85
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:30,775
There were further lotteries,
and they came every month.

86
00:05:30,775 --> 00:05:36,226
In 1943 the administration began a
Malaya-wide lottery, Konan Saiken,

87
00:05:36,306 --> 00:05:41,275
a name that roughly means "lottery
for the rise of the South".

88
00:05:41,275 --> 00:05:45,873
Its tickets still cost $1, and
the first three monthly issues of

89
00:05:45,953 --> 00:05:50,989
300,000 tickets each were reported
to have sold out within a few days,

90
00:05:51,069 --> 00:05:55,600
with tickets bought by the public
changing hands at a premium.

91
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:59,432
From the fourth issue, in August 1943,

92
00:05:59,512 --> 00:06:03,858
each lottery was raised
to 400,000 tickets,

93
00:06:03,938 --> 00:06:08,491
half of them for Singapore,
with 3,239 prizes

94
00:06:08,571 --> 00:06:13,100
worth $240,000 and a
first prize of $50,000.

95
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:16,765
Each ticket carried the
stated purpose of the money,

96
00:06:16,845 --> 00:06:20,294
printed in Chinese characters
down its two sides:

97
00:06:20,374 --> 00:06:25,200
health facilities on one side and
relief for refugees on the other.

98
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:29,600
Winners were often reported
giving some of their prize away.

99
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:33,280
The winner of an early
$50,000 first prize,

100
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:38,439
a public works employee in
Malacca, gave $5,000 to charity,

101
00:06:38,519 --> 00:06:43,861
and in October 1943 the staff
of a Syonan municipal office who

102
00:06:43,941 --> 00:06:48,750
had shared a prize gave $600
to the Japanese Red Cross.

103
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:52,383
The draws were held at the
same hall, and in May 1943

104
00:06:52,463 --> 00:06:56,377
the paper announced that those
who could not get in would

105
00:06:56,457 --> 00:07:00,100
be able to hear the results
on loudspeakers outside.

106
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:02,833
A lottery prize was a fixed sum in

107
00:07:02,912 --> 00:07:05,725
a currency that kept losing value.

108
00:07:05,725 --> 00:07:10,954
Banana money was issued at par
with the Straits dollar in 1942,

109
00:07:11,034 --> 00:07:15,084
but by August 1945 the
administration had put out

110
00:07:15,164 --> 00:07:19,635
something like $4,000 million
of it, against a pre-war

111
00:07:19,715 --> 00:07:24,350
circulation for the whole
region of about $220 million.

112
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:29,093
According to a National Archives
oral history quoted in the

113
00:07:29,173 --> 00:07:33,588
National Library's BiblioAsia
journal, an egg that cost

114
00:07:33,668 --> 00:07:38,656
3 cents before the war cost $100
by the end of the Occupation,

115
00:07:38,736 --> 00:07:43,150
and a kati of pork that had
cost 30 cents cost $1,500.

116
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:45,574
A $100,000 prize in

117
00:07:45,654 --> 00:07:49,475
September 1942 was a fortune.

118
00:07:49,475 --> 00:07:54,372
A $50,000 prize two years
later bought far less,

119
00:07:54,452 --> 00:07:57,483
and by 1945 the administration

120
00:07:57,563 --> 00:08:01,400
had introduced $100 and $1,000 notes.

121
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:04,350
The lotteries went on to the end.

122
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:09,549
On the 1st of June 1945
the 25th issue of Konan

123
00:08:09,629 --> 00:08:14,154
Saiken went on sale, now 500,000 tickets,

124
00:08:14,234 --> 00:08:19,400
still at $1 each, with
53,738 winning tickets.

125
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:23,370
Beside it ran a second
"relief lottery", Engo Saiken,

126
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:28,260
at $5 a ticket, sold at banks,
theatres, the Great World and New

127
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,925
World amusement parks, community
associations and pawnshops.

128
00:08:32,925 --> 00:08:36,320
On the 2nd of August the paper carried the

129
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:39,875
notice for the 27th issue of Konan Saiken.

130
00:08:39,875 --> 00:08:43,273
The drawing was set for
the 5th of September 1945,

131
00:08:43,353 --> 00:08:47,726
and prizes were to be paid between
the 10th of September and the

132
00:08:47,806 --> 00:08:50,787
31st of October by the
Nanpo Kaihatsu Kinko,

133
00:08:50,867 --> 00:08:56,074
the Japanese development bank that
issued the banana notes, with the warning

134
00:08:56,154 --> 00:09:00,050
that "no prize money will be
paid after the date fixed".

135
00:09:00,050 --> 00:09:02,992
Japan surrendered on the 15th of August,

136
00:09:03,072 --> 00:09:06,769
and British troops landed
back in Singapore on the

137
00:09:06,849 --> 00:09:10,475
5th of September, the day
the 27th draw was due.

138
00:09:10,475 --> 00:09:14,548
In September the British
Military Administration declared

139
00:09:14,628 --> 00:09:18,410
banana money worthless, so
even a prize that had been

140
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:22,425
paid was paid in notes that
no longer bought anything.

141
00:09:22,425 --> 00:09:25,743
The National Museum of
Singapore, which holds several

142
00:09:25,823 --> 00:09:29,206
of the tickets, notes that
many people who bought them

143
00:09:29,286 --> 00:09:32,797
reported that prizes were
never paid, and that the money

144
00:09:32,877 --> 00:09:36,275
went to fund the war and
the military administration.

145
00:09:36,275 --> 00:09:39,776
That account comes from the
people who bought the tickets,

146
00:09:39,856 --> 00:09:45,149
and the published accounts of where the money
went come from the administration itself,

147
00:09:45,229 --> 00:09:47,575
so neither can be fully checked today.

148
00:09:47,575 --> 00:09:51,141
Why it matters today: Syonan
Syoken is a small part

149
00:09:51,221 --> 00:09:55,288
of the Occupation's history,
but it shows how a government

150
00:09:55,368 --> 00:09:58,800
lottery can serve more
than one purpose at once.

151
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:02,467
The administration said
openly that it wanted to raise

152
00:10:02,547 --> 00:10:05,798
money and to soak up the
cash in people's hands,

153
00:10:05,878 --> 00:10:09,059
and it printed its good
causes on every ticket,

154
00:10:09,139 --> 00:10:13,650
while the currency the prizes were
paid in kept losing its value.

155
00:10:13,650 --> 00:10:17,333
Singapore Pools was set up in 1968 to give

156
00:10:17,413 --> 00:10:21,625
people a legal alternative
to illegal gambling.

157
00:10:21,625 --> 00:10:24,443
Its surplus now goes to the Tote Board,

158
00:10:24,523 --> 00:10:28,306
a statutory board that funds
charities and community

159
00:10:28,386 --> 00:10:32,175
projects and reports its
grants publicly each year.

160
00:10:32,175 --> 00:10:35,122
The wartime lottery is
a reminder of why that

161
00:10:35,202 --> 00:10:38,553
kind of public accounting
matters when the proceeds

162
00:10:38,633 --> 00:10:41,525
of a lottery are said
to go to good causes.
